---
import DisclaimerLine from "../../../components/banking/DisclaimerLine.astro";
import BankingShell from "../../../components/banking/BankingShell.astro";
import HowTo from "../../../components/banking/HowTo.astro";
import { REVIEWED_LINE, SBA_GUIDE_DESCRIPTION, SBA_GUIDE_STEPS } from "../../../data/banking/copy";
import { SBA_GUIDE_PATH } from "../../../data/banking/tools";
import { bankingCards } from "../../../lib/banking/downloads";
import { BANKING_CRUMB, HOME_CRUMB, breadcrumbJsonLd, graphJsonLd, webPageJsonLd } from "../../../lib/banking/seo";
import { withBase } from "../../../lib/site";

const title = "SBA 7(a) and 504 prep guide";
const card = bankingCards().find((item) => item.id === "sba-7a-504");
const file = card?.files[0];
const jumps = [
  ["what-it-is", "What an SBA loan really is"],
  ["program-7a", "The 7(a) program"],
  ["program-504", "The 504 program"],
  ["compare", "7(a) and 504 side by side"],
  ["basic-rules", "Basic rules"],
  ["documents", "Documents and forms"],
  ["steps", "The steps"],
  ["mistakes", "Common mistakes"],
  ["questions", "Questions to ask a lender"],
  ["sources", "Sources"],
];
const jsonLd = graphJsonLd([
  webPageJsonLd({ name: title, path: SBA_GUIDE_PATH, description: SBA_GUIDE_DESCRIPTION }),
  breadcrumbJsonLd([HOME_CRUMB, BANKING_CRUMB, { name: title, path: SBA_GUIDE_PATH }]),
]);
---

<BankingShell title={title} description={SBA_GUIDE_DESCRIPTION} jsonLd={jsonLd} section="templates">
  <h1 class="room-title">{title}</h1>
  <p class="lede">{SBA_GUIDE_DESCRIPTION}</p>
  <p class="bank-reviewed">{REVIEWED_LINE}</p>
  <p class="bank-callout">
    SBA rules change, sometimes several times a year. SOP 50 10 8.1 is effective October 1, 2026 for applications issued an SBA loan number on or after that date. Facts on this page were checked on SBA.gov and in SOP 50 10 8.1 on October 1, 2026. Confirm details at SBA.gov or with a lender before you rely on them.
  </p>
  {
    file && (
      <p class="bank-actions">
        <a class="bank-btn" href={withBase(file.href)} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          Preview PDF · {file.sizeLabel}
        </a>
      </p>
    )
  }
  <nav class="bank-jumps" aria-label="On this page">
    {jumps.map(([id, label]) => <a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a>)}
  </nav>
  <section class="bank-block" id="what-it-is">
    <h2 class="bank-section-title">What an SBA loan really is</h2>
    <p>SBA usually does not lend the money itself. It guarantees part of a loan made by a private lender. You still owe the full amount, and you work with the lender. For 504 loans you also work with a Certified Development Company.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="bank-block" id="program-7a">
    <h2 class="bank-section-title">The 7(a) program at a glance</h2>
    <p>7(a) is the flexible program: real estate, equipment, working capital, refinancing business debt, or buying a business. SBA listed a $5 million maximum for most 7(a) loans, and $500,000 for SBA Express, when this was checked on October 1, 2026. The individual 7(a) maximum is still $5 million. Rate and term are negotiated with the lender, inside SBA caps. Ask the lender for the current guaranty fee.</p>
    <p>Buying a business: SOP 50 10 8.1 puts change-of-ownership rules in Appendix 15. That appendix covers coverage standards, valuation, and Quality of Earnings for acquisitions. Those requirements can be stricter than for other loan purposes. Ask a lender early if the deal is an acquisition or an owner buyout.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="bank-block" id="program-504">
    <h2 class="bank-section-title">The 504 program at a glance</h2>
    <p>504 is for major fixed assets, such as an owner-occupied building or long-life equipment. A common structure is up to 50 percent from a private lender, up to 40 percent from a CDC loan backed by an SBA-guaranteed debenture, and at least 10 percent from the borrower. It is not for working capital or inventory.</p>
    <p>Standard projects are generally capped at a $5 million aggregate gross debenture. Small manufacturers and certain energy public policy projects can reach $5.5 million per eligible project. That limit applies to the SBA-backed CDC portion. Confirm current limits with a CDC.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="bank-block" id="compare">
    <h2 class="bank-section-title">7(a) and 504 side by side</h2>
    <p>Choose 7(a) when the project mixes uses or includes working capital or a business purchase. Choose 504 when the project is a major fixed asset and you want a fixed rate on the CDC portion. Some projects use both.</p>
    <p>Under Policy Notice 5000-879058 (effective July 4, 2026, and folded into SOP 50 10 8.1), outstanding 7(a) generally does not reduce 504 debenture capacity when the 7(a) is approved first, then the 504. The individual 7(a) maximum is still $5 million. A lender or CDC can walk through the tradeoffs.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="bank-block" id="basic-rules">
    <h2 class="bank-section-title">Basic rules</h2>
    <p>Both programs start from the same basic rules: a for-profit operating business in the United States, small under SBA size standards (count affiliates), a sound use of the money, and a reasonable ability to repay. Meeting those rules does not mean approval.</p>
    <p>Under SOP 50 10 8.1, all direct and indirect owners and SBA-required guarantors generally must be U.S. citizens or U.S. nationals whose principal residence is in the United States, its territories, or possessions.</p>
    <p>Startups (about one year of revenue or less) and many business purchases generally need at least 10 percent equity. Acquisition details vary by transaction type in Appendix 15. For 504 projects, SBA describes at least 10 percent from the borrower. Use the <a href={withBase("/banking/calculators/sba-quick-check/")}>SBA quick-check</a> to spot obvious issues, then confirm with a lender.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="bank-block" id="documents">
    <h2 class="bank-section-title">Documents and SBA forms</h2>
    <p>Expect tax returns, financial statements, a debt schedule, and a personal financial statement, plus SBA forms your lender names. Common ones are Form 1919 for 7(a) (current version effective March 19, 2025), Form 1244 for 504, and Form 413 for the personal financial statement (current version effective February 13, 2025). Those versions were checked on October 1, 2026. Download forms from SBA.gov or get them from your lender. The <a href={withBase("/banking/templates/#loan-package-checklist")}>loan package checklist</a> lists the financial documents by loan type.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="bank-block" id="steps">
    <h2 class="bank-section-title">The steps</h2>
    <ol class="bank-steps">
      <li>Get your numbers in order: tax returns, year-to-date financials, debt schedule, and a personal financial statement.</li>
      <li>Talk with your own lender early about whether SBA or a conventional loan fits.</li>
      <li>The lender reviews the file, orders any third-party reports, and either uses delegated authority or submits the request to SBA.</li>
      <li>Closing waits on documents, insurance, and those reports. Ask the lender for a realistic timeline for your deal.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>
  <section class="bank-block" id="mistakes">
    <h2 class="bank-section-title">Common mistakes</h2>
    <ul class="bank-reasons">
      <li>Sending tax returns with schedules missing.</li>
      <li>Signing a purchase contract with a short deadline before talking with a lender.</li>
      <li>Asking for add-backs you cannot explain.</li>
      <li>Leaving out debts, guarantees, or an older government loan.</li>
      <li>Forgetting closing costs or a construction contingency.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section class="bank-block" id="questions">
    <h2 class="bank-section-title">Questions to ask a lender</h2>
    <ul class="bank-reasons">
      <li>Do you make SBA loans often, and do you have delegated authority?</li>
      <li>Is 7(a), 504, or a conventional loan a better fit, and why?</li>
      <li>Is the rate fixed or variable, and is there a prepayment fee?</li>
      <li>What collateral and personal guarantees do you need?</li>
      <li>How much of my own money has to go in, and what is a realistic timeline?</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section class="bank-block" id="sources">
    <h2 class="bank-section-title">Sources</h2>
    <ul class="bank-reasons">
      <li><a href="https://www.sba.gov/funding-programs/loans/7a-loans">SBA 7(a) loans</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://www.sba.gov/funding-programs/loans/504-loans">SBA 504 loans</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://www.sba.gov/partners/lenders/7a-loan-program/terms-conditions-eligibility">SBA lender program terms</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://www.sba.gov/document/sop-50-10-lender-development-company-loan-programs">SOP 50 10 8.1</a> (effective October 1, 2026)</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.sba.gov/document/information-notice-5000-880695-issuance-sop-50-10-81">Information Notice 5000-880695</a> (issuance of SOP 50 10 8.1)</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.sba.gov/document/policy-notice-5000-879058-coordination-7a-504-maximum-loan-limits">Policy Notice 5000-879058</a> (7(a) and 504 maximum loan limits)</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.sba.gov/document/procedural-notice-5000-876626-revised-applicant-ownership-citizenship-residency-requirements-7a-504-loans">Procedural Notice 5000-876626</a> (citizenship and residency)</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-13/chapter-I/part-120">13 CFR Part 120</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <HowTo steps={SBA_GUIDE_STEPS} />
  <DisclaimerLine />
</BankingShell>
